    {
      "diff": "@@ -2913,6 +2913,80 @@ upon the conversation index and values inside the request packets.\n     }\n \n \n+2.2.6 Registering with Analyze → Conversation Filter\n+\n+If your protocol defines conversations (e.g., using `conversation_new()`), you can register it with the “Analyze → Conversation Filter” menu in Wireshark.\n+\n+To do this, use the `conversation_filter_add()` function declared in `epan/conversation_filter.h`. This allows users to quickly apply a display filter to traffic belonging to that conversation from the GUI.\n+\n+Basic example:\n+\n+```c\n+conversation_t *conversation = find_or_create_conversation(pinfo);\n+conversation_filter_add(conversation, \"myproto\");\n+\n+\n+2.2.7 Initialization and Cleanup in Dissector Plugins\n+\n+Dissectors can define optional functions for initialization and cleanup purposes,\n+especially useful when managing resources such as memory arenas.\n+\n+- proto_register_foo() should be used to register protocol fields and can\n+  also perform global (once-only) initialization.\n+- proto_reg_handoff_foo() is called after registration and is typically used\n+  to register the dissector with heuristic or static tables.\n+\n+There are no built-in dissect_init() or dissect_cleanup() hooks. If your plugin\n+needs to allocate temporary memory per-packet or per-frame, use the pinfo->pool\n+which is an ephemeral memory pool managed by Wireshark and reset after each packet.\n+\n+Example:\n+\n+    static int proto_foo = -1;\n+\n+    void proto_register_foo(void) {\n+        static hf_register_info hf[] = {\n+            { &hf_foo_field,\n+              { \"Foo Field\", \"foo.field\",\n+                FT_UINT8, BASE_DEC, NULL, 0x0,\n+                NULL, HFILL }\n+            }\n+        };\n+        proto_foo = proto_register_protocol(\"Foo Protocol\", \"FOO\", \"foo\");\n+        proto_register_field_array(proto_foo, hf, array_length(hf));\n+    }\n+\n+    void proto_reg_handoff_foo(void) {\n+        dissector_handle_t foo_handle;\n+\n+        foo_handle = create_dissector_handle(dissect_foo, proto_foo);\n+        dissector_add_uint(\"udp.port\", FOO_PORT, foo_handle);\n+    }\n+\n+\n+2.2.8 Exported PDUs\n+\n+Wireshark supports Exported PDUs (Protocol Data Units), which allow pre-processed or extracted protocol payloads\n+to be re-encapsulated and interpreted as individual packets. This is useful for cases where an external tool extracts\n+sessions (e.g., VoIP, HTTP) and reinjects them into a capture for post-analysis.\n+\n+To handle Exported PDUs, Wireshark uses the `exported_pdu` dissector, which can wrap protocols like TCP, UDP, or any\n+other payload into a new frame.\n+\n+An Exported PDU typically includes the following fields:\n+\n+- `pdu_type`: The protocol type of the encapsulated PDU.\n+- `tvb`: The data buffer containing the payload.\n+- `metadata`: (Optional) Custom information passed to the dissector.\n+\n+Plugins or dissectors can create and register exported PDUs using the internal API or by writing their data in PCAPNG\n+format with `block_type` `0x00000BAD`.\n+\n+This mechanism improves modular analysis and allows tools to reuse Wireshark’s decoding logic.\n+\n+For more, see: `epan/exported_pdu.h`\n+\n+\n \n 2.3 Dynamic conversation dissector registration.\n \n",
